; 29-postfix.kurt

; `postfix OP lbp` declares `OP` as a postfix operator (applied after its
; single argument), the mirror image of `prefix` (28-prefix.kurt).

postfix "!" 100

var a
parse a!                ; (! a)

; As always, declaring the syntax gives no meaning -- `a!` doesn't compute a
; factorial by itself.  You'd add that separately, e.g. via `use` axioms
; relating `a!` to `a`, `-`, and `*`.
